 Since $x^3 - 3x + 5$ is divided by $x + 2',$ we must have \[x^3 - 3x + 5 = (cx + d)(x + 2) + e\]for some constants $c,$ $d,$ and $e.$  Setting $x = -2,$ we get  \[-8 + 6 + 5 = e \implies e = 3.\]Setting $x = 0,$ we get  \[5 = 2d + e \implies d = 1.\]

Then \[x^3 - 3x + 5 = (cx + 1)(x + 2) + 3\]for all $x.$  Setting $x = 1,$ we get  \[3 = c + 3 + 3 \implies c = -3.\]

Then \[x^3 - 3x + 5 = -(x^2 - x - (3x - 3)) + 3 \]for all $x.$  Setting $x = -2,$ we get  \[(-2)^3 - 3(-2) + 5 = -24 + d - (3d - 3) + 3 \implies d = 1.\]Thus, we have  \[x^3 - 3x + 5 = -(x^2 - x - 3) + 3\]which reduces to  \[x^3 - 3x + 5 = -x^2 + x + 3 = -(x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12})).\]We can then write  \[(x + (1 - i\sqrt{12}))(x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12})) = x^3 - 3x + 5.\]By the Factor Theorem, the roots $1 + i\sqrt{12}$ and $1 - i\sqrt{12}$ of $x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12}) = 0$ are the roots of the denominator $x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12}).$

Thus, 
\[x^3 - 3x + 5 = -x^2 + x + 3 = -(x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12})) = -(x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12})) = -\overline{x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12})} = -\overline{(x + (1 - i\sqrt{12}))(x^2 - (1 + i\sqrt{12})x - (1 - i\sqrt{12}))}\\ = -\overline{x^3 - 3x + 5} = -x^3 + 3x - 5.\][asy]
